.TH CCOL_CIRCQ_SEND_ZC 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_circq_send_zc \- send a message to the queue (it blocks)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_circq_send_zc(ccol_circular_queue *cq, c_message_t *msg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function blocks until the queue has free space and then transfers
the ownership of the message data to the queue. On success it sets the
msg->data pointer to NULL; this is the zero-copy behaviour.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
cq   Circular queue to send to
msg  Message to send. On success the ownership of the data moves to the
     queue.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       - The function sent the message
ccol_invalid_args  - cq or msg is NULL, or the check of msg fails
ccol_not_permitted - Sends on the queue are turned off
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function blocks with no time limit while it waits for free space,
  or for sends on the queue to go off.
After the function wakes up, it checks again whether sends are on.
On failure, the caller keeps the ownership of msg->data.
msg->data == NULL with msg->size == 0 is valid (a sentinel message).
msg->data == NULL with msg->size > 0 is not valid.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circq_try_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_timed_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_circq_recv_zc (3)
